--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Speaker separation models (CC BY 4.0)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Both models are licensed under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
International licence (CC BY 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/

Changes made: converted to Core ML (FP16, fixed 10 second windows); the
segmentation model's SincNet filters are precomputed; the segmentation model
outputs logits (Vauta applies the log-softmax); the embedding model's masked
statistics pooling is fused into the network with the masks and a fixed
589-to-125 frame resampling, and its numerical epsilon is 1e-6. The learned
parameters are otherwise unchanged. The conversion script is
tools/convert-diarization-models; the source repository, revision and
checkpoint SHA-256 are also written into the metadata of each bundled model.
The original authors do not endorse Vauta.

1. Speaker segmentation: the pyannote community-1 segmentation model.
   Authors: Alexis Plaquet and Hervé Bredin (pyannote.audio).
   Source: https://huggingface.co/pyannote/speaker-diarization-community-1
   (file segmentation/pytorch_model.bin, revision
   3533c8cf8e369892e6b79ff1bf80f7b0286a54ee, SHA-256
   7ad24338d844fb95985486eb1a464e32d229f6d7a03c9abe60f978bacf3f816e).
   Reference: A. Plaquet and H. Bredin, "Powerset multi-class cross entropy
   loss for neural speaker diarization", Interspeech 2023.
   Licence: CC BY 4.0, as stated on the model card. Upstream, the model is
   gated on Hugging Face (users accept the repository's conditions before
   downloading); CC BY 4.0 allows redistribution with attribution, which this
   notice and the metadata of the bundled model provide.

2. Speaker embedding: WeSpeaker ResNet34-LM, trained on VoxCeleb.
   Authors: the WeSpeaker authors (Hongji Wang, Chengdong Liang, Shuai Wang,
   Zhengyang Chen, Binbin Zhang, Xu Xiang, Yanlei Deng and Yanmin Qian),
   distributed for pyannote.audio by Hervé Bredin.
   Source: https://huggingface.co/pyannote/wespeaker-voxceleb-resnet34-LM
   (file pytorch_model.bin, revision 837717ddb9ff5507820346191109dc79c958d614,
   SHA-256 366edf44f4c80889a3eb7a9d7bdf02c4aede3127f7dd15e274dcdb826b143c56).
   Reference: H. Wang et al., "Wespeaker: A research and production oriented
   speaker embedding learning toolkit", ICASSP 2023.

Test data: Tests/VautaCoreTests/Fixtures/Diarization contains a 12 second
excerpt of the AMI Meeting Corpus (J. Carletta et al.,
https://groups.inf.ed.ac.uk/ami/corpus/, CC BY 4.0), adapted for testing
(speakers mixed, level-normalised, Opus-coded, trimmed, 16-bit PCM), and
numbers computed from it. It is not part of the app.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Evaluation data (eval/heldout)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The text files under eval/heldout (reference turns and scored regions, build
descriptions, Spanish reference texts) are derived from third-party corpora and
are not covered by the Apache License; each keeps its own licence:

- AMI Meeting Corpus (J. Carletta et al.), annotations via pyannote/AMI-diarization-setup:
  CC BY 4.0.
- VoxConverse annotations (J. S. Chung et al.): CC BY 4.0. The audio is not in
  this repository and its copyright stays with its owners.
- Multilingual LibriSpeech Spanish (V. Pratap et al., Meta AI), reference texts:
  CC BY 4.0.
- VoxPopuli Spanish (C. Wang et al., Meta AI), reference texts: CC0 1.0.

Changes made: turns and regions cut, merged or time-shifted to match the
evaluation audio; texts joined in the order the audio was built. See
eval/heldout/README.md and eval/heldout/sets/es_wer/ATTRIBUTION.md. No audio is
committed.
